<article class="env-lemma" id="05GQ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05GQ">Lemma <span data-tag="05GQ">15.15.4</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$R$ has property (P) of Lemma <a href="/tag/05GN" data-tag="05GN">15.15.2</a>, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
any injective map of projective $R$-modules is universally injective, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $u : N \to M$ is injective and $N$, $M$ are finite projective $R$-modules then $\mathop{\mathrm{Coker}}(u)$ is a finite projective $R$-module, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
if $N \subset M$ and $N$, $M$ are finite projective as $R$-modules, then $N$ is a direct summand of $M$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
any injective map $R \to R^{\oplus n}$ is a split injection. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication (1) $\Rightarrow $ (2) is Lemma <a href="/tag/05GP" data-tag="05GP">15.15.3</a>. It is clear that (3) and (4) are equivalent. We have (2) $\Rightarrow $ (3), (4) by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/058L" data-tag="058L">10.82.4</a>. Part (5) is a special case of (4). Assume (5). Let $I = (a_1, \ldots , a_ n)$ be a proper finitely generated ideal of $R$. As $I \not= R$ we see that $R \to R^{\oplus n}$, $x \mapsto (xa_1, \ldots , xa_ n)$ is not a split injection. Hence it has a nonzero kernel and we conclude that $\text{Ann}_ R(I) \not= 0$. Thus (1) holds. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>